1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,156
A merge stops. In the file there's a block
with two candidate lines: one ends in

2
00:00:09,196 --> 00:00:13,911
.strip(), the other in .lower(). An engineer
looks at the two, decides the second

3
00:00:13,951 --> 00:00:16,495
looks newer, deletes
the first, and commits.

4
00:00:16,834 --> 00:00:21,227
The correct answer was neither line.
It was both normalizations together.

5
00:00:21,267 --> 00:00:26,206
And nothing in the block could have told
them, because the block did not show what

6
00:00:26,246 --> 00:00:30,941
the line had been before either side
touched it. Hold that block in your mind.

7
00:00:30,981 --> 00:00:33,006
You'll face the same choice later.

8
00:00:35,346 --> 00:00:40,828
The textbook cites a poll in which 61 percent
of roughly 1,480 respondents had seen

9
00:00:40,868 --> 00:00:45,286
a production bug caused by a bad
conflict resolution at least once.

10
00:00:45,326 --> 00:00:50,808
It's a self-selected sample, as it notes.
Most bad resolutions have the same cause:

11
00:00:50,848 --> 00:00:55,599
someone guessed from the markers. A
conflict is not two lines in a file.

12
00:00:55,639 --> 00:01:00,456
It is a merge that stopped halfway and
wrote its state into three places.

13
00:01:00,496 --> 00:01:02,252
This video reads all three.

14
00:01:04,092 --> 00:01:07,717
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:07,757 --> 00:01:12,644
A merge combines two branches, and a conflict
is the point where Git can't combine

16
00:01:12,684 --> 00:01:17,331
two edits and asks you. In the last video
you learned when two edits conflict.

17
00:01:17,371 --> 00:01:22,258
Now the merge has stopped, and the question
is: what exactly did Git leave behind,

18
00:01:22,298 --> 00:01:23,640
and how do you read it?

19
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:28,749
We go through one conflicted merge, one
snippet at a time: the working tree, the

20
00:01:28,789 --> 00:01:32,655
index with its three stages, and
the files in the dot git folder.

21
00:01:32,695 --> 00:01:37,644
You met the stages briefly in video 18.
Then we see the same conflict written three

22
00:01:37,684 --> 00:01:42,693
times, in the three conflict styles, and
you'll see why the hook's engineer couldn't

23
00:01:42,733 --> 00:01:44,797
have got it right with the default.

24
00:01:45,137 --> 00:01:49,796
The full resolution workflow, with abort,
continue and quit, is the next video.

25
00:01:49,836 --> 00:01:54,316
Today we resolve one conflict the plain
way, to see the anatomy close again.

26
00:01:56,156 --> 00:01:57,734
Here's what you'll walk away with.

27
00:01:58,074 --> 00:01:59,487
After this video you can:

28
00:01:59,827 --> 00:02:03,144
List everything Git writes when
a merge stops at a conflict.

29
00:02:03,484 --> 00:02:07,517
Read stages 1, 2 and 3 of a path
without touching the working tree.

30
00:02:07,857 --> 00:02:12,300
Read conflict markers in the
merge, diff3 and zdiff3 styles.

31
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:24,264
Explain which commands are blocked
while the merge is in progress.

32
00:02:26,104 --> 00:02:31,083
In one sentence. A conflicted merge is a
merge that stopped halfway and wrote its

33
00:02:31,123 --> 00:02:36,288
unfinished state into three places: marker
blocks in working-tree files, up to three

34
00:02:36,328 --> 00:02:40,377
index entries per conflicted path,
and a handful of files in .git.

35
00:02:40,417 --> 00:02:45,334
A reminder: the working tree is your files
on disk, and the index is the staging

36
00:02:45,374 --> 00:02:47,627
area, Git's draft of the next commit.

37
00:02:47,967 --> 00:02:51,857
Precisely. The manual lists what
happens when a merge cannot complete.

38
00:02:52,197 --> 00:02:54,784
One: "The HEAD pointer stays the same."

39
00:02:55,124 --> 00:02:58,082
Two: MERGE_HEAD is set to
the other branch's tip.

40
00:02:58,422 --> 00:03:02,945
Three: paths that merged cleanly are updated
in the index and in the working tree.

41
00:03:03,285 --> 00:03:08,573
Four: for conflicting paths, the index
records up to three versions: "stage 1 stores

42
00:03:08,613 --> 00:03:13,202
the version from the common ancestor,
stage 2 from HEAD, and stage 3 from

43
00:03:13,242 --> 00:03:18,466
MERGE_HEAD". The working-tree files contain
the merge result with conflict markers.

44
00:03:18,806 --> 00:03:23,688
Five: AUTO_MERGE points to a tree that
matches what was written to the working tree.

45
00:03:24,028 --> 00:03:25,857
Six: nothing else changes.

46
00:03:26,197 --> 00:03:30,491
Quick quiz. Asha checks out your
branch and merges main into it.

47
00:03:30,531 --> 00:03:34,350
During her merge, are your
commits A, ours, or B, theirs?

48
00:03:34,390 --> 00:03:35,162
Your answer?

49
00:03:40,002 --> 00:03:45,305
Ours and theirs, exactly. In a merge, ours
is stage 2: the commit you were on when

50
00:03:45,345 --> 00:03:49,279
you typed git merge. Theirs is
stage 3: the commit you named.

51
00:03:49,319 --> 00:03:54,491
It has nothing to do with who wrote what.
So the answer is A: if Asha checks out

52
00:03:54,531 --> 00:03:58,400
your branch and merges main,
your commits are "ours" to her.

53
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:02,635
And during a rebase, the roles are
swapped relative to intuition.

54
00:04:02,675 --> 00:04:07,846
The textbook quotes a poll in which 48
percent of 1,511 respondents did not know

55
00:04:07,886 --> 00:04:08,172
that.

56
00:04:08,512 --> 00:04:13,942
Reading the stages. The syntax :<stage>:<path>
names the three blobs, so any command

57
00:04:13,982 --> 00:04:18,045
that reads objects can read them.
git show :1:path is the base.

58
00:04:18,085 --> 00:04:22,538
:2: is ours. :3: is theirs. Each is
a complete file, with no markers.

59
00:04:22,578 --> 00:04:25,469
Reading them does not
touch the working tree.

60
00:04:25,809 --> 00:04:29,481
The files in .git. MERGE_HEAD
is the commit being merged.

61
00:04:29,521 --> 00:04:32,803
It's what will make the
next commit a merge commit.

62
00:04:32,843 --> 00:04:38,275
HEAD has not moved, and ORIG_HEAD equals
it. MERGE_MSG is the prepared message, with

63
00:04:38,315 --> 00:04:43,551
the conflicted paths as a comment. MERGE_MODE
is empty unless you passed --no-ff.

64
00:04:43,591 --> 00:04:48,827
AUTO_MERGE is a tree: the snapshot of what
Git wrote to the working tree, markers

65
00:04:48,867 --> 00:04:49,413
included.

66
00:04:49,753 --> 00:04:54,824
The diff during a conflict. git diff prints
a combined diff, one column per side.

67
00:04:54,864 --> 00:04:59,051
The first column compares the
working-tree file with ours, stage 2.

68
00:04:59,091 --> 00:05:04,162
The second compares it with theirs, stage
3. git diff --ours, --theirs and --base

69
00:05:04,202 --> 00:05:08,957
compare the working-tree file with one
stage in the ordinary two-way format.

70
00:05:08,997 --> 00:05:14,194
And git diff AUTO_MERGE shows exactly what
your hand edits changed relative to what

71
00:05:14,234 --> 00:05:14,825
Git wrote.

72
00:05:15,165 --> 00:05:19,803
What is blocked. While the merge is open,
Git refuses the two things that would lose

73
00:05:19,843 --> 00:05:23,479
track of it. git commit is refused
while there are unmerged files.

74
00:05:23,519 --> 00:05:26,431
The second refusal you will
read from the transcript.

75
00:05:26,771 --> 00:05:33,090
Conflict styles. In one sentence: the
conflict style decides how much of the three

76
00:05:33,130 --> 00:05:38,441
versions is written between the
markers. merge shows ours and theirs.

77
00:05:38,481 --> 00:05:44,878
diff3 adds the base. zdiff3 adds the base,
while moving lines that both sides share

78
00:05:44,918 --> 00:05:47,204
at the edges out of the block.

79
00:05:47,544 --> 00:05:52,043
merge.conflictStyle selects the
style, and merge is the default.

80
00:05:52,083 --> 00:05:56,794
zdiff3 is available since Git 2.35,
and the textbook recommends it.

81
00:05:56,834 --> 00:06:02,467
git checkout --conflict=<style> <path>
and git restore --conflict=<style> <path>

82
00:06:02,507 --> 00:06:07,785
rewrite one conflicted file in another
style from the index stages, without

83
00:06:07,825 --> 00:06:09,204
repeating the merge.

84
00:06:09,544 --> 00:06:14,498
How to read a three-part block. Compare
each side with the base, not the sides with

85
00:06:14,538 --> 00:06:19,433
each other. A side that equals the base
did nothing in this region: take the other

86
00:06:19,473 --> 00:06:24,187
side. An empty base means both sides added
text at the same place: usually keep

87
00:06:24,227 --> 00:06:28,941
both. Two sides that each differ from the
base are two intents, and you need to

88
00:06:28,981 --> 00:06:29,603
serve both.

89
00:06:29,943 --> 00:06:35,523
Risk labels for the resolution steps. git
add <path> on an unmerged path is 🟢 SAFE:

90
00:06:35,563 --> 00:06:41,009
it collapses stages 1 to 3 into stage 0.
Remember from video 18 that git add does

91
00:06:41,049 --> 00:06:46,358
not judge a resolution. git merge --continue
is 🟡 CAUTION: it creates the merge

92
00:06:46,398 --> 00:06:51,911
commit and moves the branch. Its preview
is git diff --cached --check, and running

93
00:06:51,951 --> 00:06:57,194
the tests. git config set is 🟡 CAUTION.
The two red commands of this video are

94
00:06:57,234 --> 00:07:00,918
introduced with their five
questions where they appear.

95
00:07:02,758 --> 00:07:06,076
A picture helps. The textbook's
analogy: a customs desk.

96
00:07:06,116 --> 00:07:09,255
Everything that passed
inspection is already through.

97
00:07:09,295 --> 00:07:13,813
Each contested parcel sits on the counter
with three documents: the original

98
00:07:13,853 --> 00:07:16,451
declaration, your version
and their version.

99
00:07:16,791 --> 00:07:21,034
That picture corrects the usual one.
A conflicted file is mostly finished.

100
00:07:21,074 --> 00:07:25,779
Only the marked regions are open, and for
each open item you have three documents,

101
00:07:25,819 --> 00:07:26,242
not two.

102
00:07:26,582 --> 00:07:31,233
The analogy breaks on time: nothing here
expires until you conclude the merge or

103
00:07:31,273 --> 00:07:35,806
abort it. You're not under pressure from
Git. Breathe, and read the documents.

104
00:07:37,646 --> 00:07:42,962
Left: the working tree. One file with one
marker block, and one file fully merged.

105
00:07:43,002 --> 00:07:48,254
Middle: the index. The conflicted path
appears three times, at stages 1, 2 and 3,

106
00:07:48,294 --> 00:07:52,957
with three blob IDs, and has no stage
0. The other paths are at stage 0.

107
00:07:52,997 --> 00:07:58,313
Right: HEAD, which has not moved. Look at
stage 2 and at HEAD's entry for the same

108
00:07:58,353 --> 00:08:03,605
path: the same blob, 0e8b97c. Stage 2 is
ours. Underneath: the four files in .git

109
00:08:03,645 --> 00:08:05,826
that say "a merge is in progress".

110
00:08:06,166 --> 00:08:10,662
Four marker lines, three sections. In the
default style, the second marker and the

111
00:08:10,702 --> 00:08:12,045
base section are missing.

112
00:08:13,885 --> 00:08:17,228
Into the lab. You set the
temperature to 0.0 on main.

113
00:08:17,268 --> 00:08:22,464
Asha set it to 0.7 on her branch, changed
the seed in the same commit, and added a

114
00:08:22,504 --> 00:08:27,571
prompt line in another. git merge 🟡 CAUTION.
Predict, with the rule table: which

115
00:08:27,611 --> 00:08:31,594
paths merge cleanly, and which
line conflicts? Say it out loud.

116
00:08:36,434 --> 00:08:38,004
The exit status is 1.

117
00:08:38,344 --> 00:08:42,303
git status separates what
is done from what is open.

118
00:08:42,343 --> 00:08:46,611
prompts/judge.txt merged
cleanly, and is already staged.

119
00:08:46,651 --> 00:08:49,226
Only config/eval.yaml is unmerged.

120
00:08:49,566 --> 00:09:13,913
The working-tree copy. Between <<<<<<<
HEAD and ======= is our version of the

121
00:09:13,953 --> 00:09:35,767
region. Between ======= and >>>>>>>
feature/creative-judge is theirs.

122
00:09:35,807 --> 00:09:59,204
The last line, seed: 1234, is her seed
change, merged outside the markers.

123
00:09:59,544 --> 00:10:06,670
Now the index. Predict how many
entries config/eval.yaml has.

124
00:10:07,010 --> 00:10:12,233
The third column is the stage.
Merged paths are at stage 0.

125
00:10:12,273 --> 00:10:19,190
The conflicted path has no stage 0 entry
and three others: c5b3327 at stage 1,

126
00:10:19,230 --> 00:10:22,668
0e8b97c at stage 2, db5c556 at stage 3.

127
00:10:23,008 --> 00:10:28,333
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper. Write
the three git show commands that print

128
00:10:28,373 --> 00:10:33,631
the base, ours and theirs of config/eval.yaml,
without touching the working tree.

129
00:10:33,671 --> 00:10:35,136
Then say them out loud.

130
00:10:42,976 --> 00:10:48,566
The answer: git show :1:config/eval.yaml
for the base, then :2: for ours and :3: for

131
00:10:48,606 --> 00:10:53,391
theirs. After the base come two diffs:
base to ours, and base to theirs.

132
00:10:53,431 --> 00:10:58,954
The textbook calls those two diffs the most
useful commands in conflict resolution:

133
00:10:58,994 --> 00:11:04,449
they show what each side intended. Here
they reveal something the marker block did

134
00:11:04,489 --> 00:11:07,398
not: their side changed
two lines, ours one.

135
00:11:10,738 --> 00:11:16,660
Inside .git: AUTO_MERGE, MERGE_HEAD,
MERGE_MODE, MERGE_MSG, ORIG_HEAD.

136
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:20,469
The combined diff: two columns
of markers before each line.

137
00:11:20,509 --> 00:11:25,405
A + in both columns marks a line that neither
side has, which here means the marker

138
00:11:25,445 --> 00:11:25,762
lines.

139
00:11:26,102 --> 00:11:30,900
git log --merge narrows the two sides to
the commits that touched the conflicted

140
00:11:30,940 --> 00:11:35,738
path: one of yours, one of hers. The commit
that only added a prompt line is not

141
00:11:35,778 --> 00:11:36,161
listed.

142
00:11:36,501 --> 00:11:40,028
Now try to walk away. Predict
what git commit says.

143
00:11:40,068 --> 00:11:41,147
Say it out loud.

144
00:11:45,987 --> 00:11:49,820
Refused: "Committing is not possible
because you have unmerged files."

145
00:11:49,860 --> 00:11:54,357
Read the second refusal in the same snippet:
it is the other thing that would lose

146
00:11:54,397 --> 00:11:55,408
track of the merge.

147
00:11:58,748 --> 00:12:03,309
The resolution, in an editor: delete the
three marker lines and the 0.7 line.

148
00:12:03,349 --> 00:12:08,207
git diff AUTO_MERGE shows exactly what the
hand edit changed, relative to what Git

149
00:12:08,247 --> 00:12:08,566
wrote.

150
00:12:08,906 --> 00:12:13,531
git add 🟢 SAFE on the unmerged path:
three entries become one at stage 0.

151
00:12:13,571 --> 00:12:17,110
Status: "All conflicts fixed
but you are still merging."

152
00:12:17,450 --> 00:12:22,112
git merge --continue 🟡 CAUTION
creates the merge commit, a94e9f6.

153
00:12:22,152 --> 00:12:25,728
The MERGE_* files are gone,
and ORIG_HEAD remains.

154
00:12:26,068 --> 00:12:31,051
And the message says why 0.0 was kept. A
resolution is a decision, and the merge

155
00:12:31,091 --> 00:12:33,877
commit's message is where
the reason belongs.

156
00:12:34,217 --> 00:12:40,909
Both branches added the same guard against
missing predictions, and then changed the

157
00:12:40,949 --> 00:12:42,753
comparison differently.

158
00:12:46,093 --> 00:12:51,015
The default style. Two candidate lines,
and no hint of what the line was before.

159
00:12:51,055 --> 00:12:56,101
Did one side add .strip() and the other
.lower(), or did someone remove something?

160
00:12:56,141 --> 00:13:01,002
This is the block from the hook. Decide
now what you would keep, from this view

161
00:13:01,042 --> 00:13:02,429
alone. Say it out loud.

162
00:13:07,269 --> 00:13:12,094
The next two commands rewrite the
conflicted file from the index stages.

163
00:13:12,134 --> 00:13:17,501
git checkout --conflict=<style> and git
restore --conflict=<style> belong to the

164
00:13:17,541 --> 00:13:22,975
group the textbook labels 🔴 DANGEROUS:
restoring a conflicted path overwrites the

165
00:13:23,015 --> 00:13:24,191
working-tree file.

166
00:13:24,531 --> 00:13:28,747
The five questions. What it changes:
the working-tree file only.

167
00:13:28,787 --> 00:13:34,134
What it can destroy: your hand edits to
that file, which exist nowhere else until

168
00:13:34,174 --> 00:13:38,855
you git add them. How to preview: git
diff --ours or git diff --theirs.

169
00:13:38,895 --> 00:13:44,375
How to recover: git restore --merge <path>
brings back the starting point, not your

170
00:13:44,415 --> 00:13:49,961
edits. When it is appropriate: to restart
a resolution, or, as here, before you have

171
00:13:50,001 --> 00:13:51,025
edited anything.

172
00:13:51,365 --> 00:13:54,801
In diff3 style, the section
after ||||||| is the base.

173
00:13:54,841 --> 00:14:00,015
And it settles the question from the hook:
the original compared pred == gold, so

174
00:14:00,055 --> 00:14:04,457
each side added one normalization,
and the right answer applies both.

175
00:14:04,497 --> 00:14:09,736
The price is a larger block: the guard
lines that both sides added identically are

176
00:14:09,776 --> 00:14:11,474
inside the conflict, twice.

177
00:14:11,814 --> 00:14:16,939
zdiff3 keeps the base and removes that noise:
the shared guard lines are outside the

178
00:14:16,979 --> 00:14:21,981
block. The labels read ours, base and
theirs, because these two files were rebuilt

179
00:14:22,021 --> 00:14:25,179
from index stages, and stages
carry no branch names.

180
00:14:25,519 --> 00:14:30,422
Last: make it the setting, and merge
afresh. That needs git merge --abort first,

181
00:14:30,462 --> 00:14:35,366
which is 🔴 DANGEROUS. What it changes:
the index, the working tree and the merge

182
00:14:35,406 --> 00:14:40,434
state. What it can destroy: every resolution
made so far, and any uncommitted edit

183
00:14:40,474 --> 00:14:42,473
that was staged during the merge.

184
00:14:42,813 --> 00:14:47,996
How to preview: git status. How to recover:
staged content survives as unreferenced

185
00:14:48,036 --> 00:14:52,275
objects that git fsck lists, and
unstaged resolution edits are gone.

186
00:14:52,315 --> 00:14:57,497
When it is appropriate: when the merge was
a mistake or needs a different approach.

187
00:14:57,537 --> 00:14:59,448
Here nothing has been resolved.

188
00:14:59,788 --> 00:15:04,727
git config set merge.conflictStyle
zdiff3 🟡 CAUTION, and a fresh merge.

189
00:15:04,767 --> 00:15:08,654
The base is now labelled with
its abbreviated commit ID.

190
00:15:10,494 --> 00:15:12,142
Five mistakes to watch for.

191
00:15:12,482 --> 00:15:17,366
Resolving from the two candidate lines
alone. Root cause: the default style shows

192
00:15:17,406 --> 00:15:22,410
ours and theirs and hides the base, so you
cannot tell what each side changed from.

193
00:15:22,750 --> 00:15:27,993
Thinking "ours" means "my work". Root cause:
in a merge, ours is stage 2, the commit

194
00:15:28,033 --> 00:15:32,960
you were on; theirs is stage 3, the commit
you named; authorship plays no part.

195
00:15:33,300 --> 00:15:38,335
Treating the whole file as broken. Root
cause: overlooking that a conflicted file is

196
00:15:38,375 --> 00:15:43,229
mostly merged; only the marker blocks are
open, and other changes from their side

197
00:15:43,269 --> 00:15:44,135
are already in.

198
00:15:44,475 --> 00:15:49,432
Committing with markers left in. Root cause:
git add does not judge a resolution; it

199
00:15:49,472 --> 00:15:50,919
stages the file as it is.

200
00:15:51,259 --> 00:15:56,401
Trusting a merge tool over the repository.
Root cause: tools read the same three

201
00:15:56,441 --> 00:16:01,647
stages; when a tool and the command line
seem to disagree, git ls-files -u is the

202
00:16:01,687 --> 00:16:02,490
ground truth.

203
00:16:04,330 --> 00:16:09,452
Now, out of the lab. An evaluation team has
a function exact_match that scores model

204
00:16:09,492 --> 00:16:13,201
outputs. One engineer makes it
ignore surrounding whitespace.

205
00:16:13,241 --> 00:16:17,934
Another makes it case-insensitive. Both
also add the same guard for a missing

206
00:16:17,974 --> 00:16:21,314
prediction. The merge conflicts
on the comparison line.

207
00:16:21,354 --> 00:16:26,477
With the default style, the reviewer sees
two lines and picks one, and the published

208
00:16:26,517 --> 00:16:29,611
scores silently lose one
of the two normalizations.

209
00:16:29,651 --> 00:16:34,467
With zdiff3, the base line is on screen,
each side's intent is visible, and the

210
00:16:34,507 --> 00:16:35,819
resolution keeps both.

211
00:16:36,159 --> 00:16:40,620
The textbook's advice: set
merge.conflictStyle to zdiff3 once, globally.

212
00:16:40,660 --> 00:16:45,872
The setting changes only how conflicts are
written to your working tree, and nothing

213
00:16:45,912 --> 00:16:50,062
that is committed, so it is a
personal choice, not a team decision.

214
00:16:50,102 --> 00:16:55,126
The one thing to check is tooling that
parses conflict markers, which must accept

215
00:16:55,166 --> 00:16:56,376
the ||||||| section.

216
00:16:58,216 --> 00:17:04,918
Your turn. Do Lab 6.2, "An edit-against-edit
conflict, resolved by reading the

217
00:17:04,958 --> 00:17:08,029
stages", in lab-manual/m06-merge.md.

218
00:17:08,369 --> 00:17:13,430
Before you open the conflicted file, predict
from the two branches: how many index

219
00:17:13,470 --> 00:17:18,654
entries the conflicted path will have, and
what git show :1:, :2: and :3: will print

220
00:17:18,694 --> 00:17:23,630
for it. Decide the resolution from the
two diffs against the base, and only then

221
00:17:23,670 --> 00:17:24,501
edit the file.

222
00:17:26,341 --> 00:17:30,312
Q128: "During a conflict, what
are index stages 1, 2 and 3?

223
00:17:30,352 --> 00:17:35,546
How do you read "their" version of a
file without touching the working tree?"

224
00:17:37,886 --> 00:17:43,282
Answer out loud. A strong answer defines
each stage by the commit it comes from, not

225
00:17:43,322 --> 00:17:47,358
by the person, and gives the exact
notation that names a stage.

226
00:17:47,398 --> 00:17:52,534
It says what stage 0 is and what its
absence means, and adds the two comparisons

227
00:17:52,574 --> 00:17:54,734
that show what each side intended.

228
00:17:56,574 --> 00:18:02,454
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say: when a merge stops, HEAD stays where

229
00:18:02,494 --> 00:18:07,457
it is. MERGE_HEAD, MERGE_MSG, MERGE_MODE
and AUTO_MERGE appear in .git.

230
00:18:07,497 --> 00:18:13,235
Clean paths are staged. And each conflicted
path has entries at stage 1, the base,

231
00:18:13,275 --> 00:18:18,872
stage 2, ours, and stage 3, theirs, plus
a working-tree file with marker blocks.

232
00:18:19,212 --> 00:18:24,287
I read a stage with git show :2:path
without touching the working tree, and I

233
00:18:24,327 --> 00:18:29,734
compare each side with the base. git commit
is blocked until every path is back at

234
00:18:29,774 --> 00:18:33,852
stage 0. The merge style shows
two sides. diff3 adds the base.

235
00:18:33,892 --> 00:18:38,236
zdiff3 adds the base and moves
shared edge lines out of the block.

236
00:18:40,076 --> 00:18:43,895
Read sections 8.8 and 8.9 of Chapter 8.

237
00:18:44,235 --> 00:18:48,914
Do Exercise 6.3, Level 1, "one
conflict, step by step", in

238
00:18:48,954 --> 00:18:51,599
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

239
00:18:51,939 --> 00:18:57,981
Challenge: Exercise 6.8, Level 3, "conflict
markers in main", in the same file.

240
00:18:58,321 --> 00:19:02,957
A conflict is no longer two mysterious
lines. It's three documents and a paused

241
00:19:02,997 --> 00:19:07,930
merge, and you know where each one lives.
Do the lab, and read the stages before you

242
00:19:07,970 --> 00:19:12,429
edit. Next time: the resolution workflow,
with abort, continue and quit, and

243
00:19:12,469 --> 00:19:16,691
restoring one side. Until then, look
at the state first and type second.

244
00:19:16,731 --> 00:19:18,112
See you in the next one.
